// =====================================================================
// Row-Level Security (Modeling -> Manage roles)
// Test with Modeling -> View as -> pick role (and "Other user" for dynamic).
// =====================================================================

// ---- 1. Static RLS: one role per airline ----
// Role "American Airlines" -> table dim_airline, filter:
[airline_code] = "AA"

// ---- 2. Dynamic RLS: one role for everybody ----
// Add a security table (e.g. flights_gold.sec_user_airline in Databricks, or Enter data):
//   user_email            | airline_code
//   analyst1@company.com  | AA
//   analyst1@company.com  | DL
//   manager@company.com   | *        (see variant below)
// Option A: relate sec_user_airline -> dim_airline, filter the security table on
//   [user_email] = USERPRINCIPALNAME(), and set the relationship to "Both" with
//   "Apply security filter in both directions" (bidirectional = slower, more ambiguity risk).
//
// Option B (preferred) - no relationship, filter dim_airline directly:
// Role "Airline Users" -> table dim_airline, filter:
[airline_code]
    IN CALCULATETABLE (
        VALUES ( sec_user_airline[airline_code] ),
        sec_user_airline[user_email] = USERPRINCIPALNAME ()
    )

// Variant with "see everything" users:
VAR _codes =
    CALCULATETABLE (
        VALUES ( sec_user_airline[airline_code] ),
        sec_user_airline[user_email] = USERPRINCIPALNAME ()
    )
RETURN
    "*" IN _codes || [airline_code] IN _codes

// Notes for interviews:
// - RLS filters ROWS; Object-Level Security (OLS, via Tabular Editor) hides tables/columns.
// - RLS applies to Viewers only; workspace Admin/Member/Contributor bypass it.
// - In the Service: Semantic model -> Security -> add users/groups to the role.
